{
  "image_name": "2b6952c068e07ad9097f0708fdd97d69.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several engineering strategies for multifunctional antibody-based cancer therapeutics, centered on T-cell engagement and conditional activity. One panel shows a T-cell engager that binds a tumor-associated antigen on the cancer cell while simultaneously contacting CD3 and the co-stimulatory receptor CD28 on a T or natural killer cell, illustrating how tumor recognition can be coupled to immune activation.\n\nThe rest of the figure compares modular ways to control or assemble these molecules. Several “prodrug” concepts are depicted: activity can be masked and then unblocked by proteases in the tumor environment, steric hindrance can be removed proteolytically, and binding affinity can be switched by soluble factors such as pH or ATP. Other schemes show components assembling directly on the tumor cell or exchanging chains on the cell surface, so functional bispecific complexes form only when the right targets are present.\n\nAdditional approaches expand beyond simple binding. A PROTAC-like design links target recognition to a degrader function, suggesting selective protein degradation. Another panel shows delivery of bispecific antibody genes by a vector so the therapeutic is produced in cells. The final concept mimics cytokine signaling by bringing together two cytokine receptor subunits, indicating receptor activation through antibody-mediated clustering.",
